---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import { GLOSSARY } from '../data/glossary';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const materials = (await import('../../public/data/materials.json')).default as {
  sources: Record<string, { citation: string; url: string; doi?: string }>;
  materials: Record<string, { name: string; vp: { value: number; range: number[] | null; status: string; source: string | null }; vs: { value: number; range: number[] | null; status: string; source: string | null }; rho: { value: number; status: string } }>;
};
const fmtR = (p: { value: number; range: number[] | null }) =>
  p.value === 0 ? '0' : p.range ? `${p.range[0].toLocaleString('en-US')}–${p.range[1].toLocaleString('en-US')}` : p.value.toLocaleString('en-US');
const mark = (s: string) => (s === 'sourced' || s === 'definition' ? '' : s === 'derived' ? ' ′' : ' *');
---
<Doc
  title="Method and sources"
  kicker="Reproducibility"
  current="method"
  lede="How what we found is known, how sure each part is, and how to check it. Everything on this site is generated from the repository by code anyone can run: the compositions from their JSON, the simulations from their scripts, the figures and volumes from the runs that made them. Nothing is edited by hand, and every number carries its source or is marked as assumed."
  index={[
    { id: 'proof', label: 'How sure' },
    { id: 'materials', label: 'Materials' },
    { id: 'glossary', label: 'Glossary' },
    { id: 'run', label: 'Run it' },
    { id: 'layout', label: 'Layout' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="proof">
    <h2>Three reasons, each checked against something it could have failed, and each with its own limits</h2>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag measured">P2-03 · real and simulated</span>
        <h3>Correlating one image’s looks registers little of ground motion</h3>
        <p>Looks cut from one image share their overlapping spectrum; for whole images their correlation peaks at zero lag exactly, and for patch registration that is a strong pull toward zero, measured rather than proved. For speckle, motion shared by a patch leaves the image’s distribution unchanged.</p>
        <p class="check">Checked: the same block motion is recovered in full against a motionless twin, so the motion is in the data; a lone bright point’s envelope follows its motion (100 ± 2%); the real crop’s spectrum reproduces its own coherence to 10<sup>−9</sup>. Limit: measured for this estimator; others need their own measurement.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">P2-04, P2-05 · modelled</span>
        <h3>In our model, the chamber’s imprint is far below the reach</h3>
        <p>Static relaxation with the lab’s elastic solver gives the chamber’s imprint on the ground’s motion (6.6 × 10<sup>−5</sup> of it); the budget combines it with what P2-03 measured, allowing every advantage.</p>
        <p class="check">Checked: intact rock stays exactly still; the result changes 3.5% from a 1 m to a 0.5 m grid and not at all with domain size; Eshelby’s void with Okada’s point sources agrees within 40%; the detection threshold is confirmed by simulation (24 of 24 detected). Limit: one small chamber in uniform rock, quasi-static, at the regional microseism level; not a measurement at Giza during a pass.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">P2-06, P2-07 · algebra, checked in code</span>
        <h3>The depth axis is a frequency axis</h3>
        <p>The steering wavenumber grows linearly with slow time, so the published focusing is a Fourier transform over time: 6.76 m of depth per hertz on the real geometry, whatever lies below.</p>
        <p class="check">Checked: the pipeline’s own code reproduces the rule to the grid, folds where predicted, and draws identical profiles over a chamber at 15 m, at 30 m and over rock; run whole (P2-07), its sections with and without the chamber differ by 10<sup>−8</sup>. Limit: the scale depends on the sound wavelength, which only relabels it; the relation itself holds whatever the ground does.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">
      Graded claim by claim on the <a href={`${base}/satellite/#sure`}>satellite page</a>, which also says what would settle what is
      open. The full audit, with what is not established and the corrections adopted after an independent review, is <a href="https://github.com/ejhong/sar/blob/main/METHOD_AUDIT.md">METHOD_AUDIT.md</a>;
      every run’s parameters, code and data are linked from its details panel on the <a href={`${base}/satellite/`}>satellite page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="materials">
    <h2>Every material property is sourced, derived from a source, or marked assumed</h2>
    <p class="sub">P and S wave speeds in m/s; ′ derived from a source by a stated ratio; * assumed. Densities are assumed throughout until an open source is found.</p>
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>P wave</th><th>S wave</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          Object.entries(materials.materials).map(([, m]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{m.name}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmtR(m.vp)}{mark(m.vp.status)}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmtR(m.vs)}{mark(m.vs.status)}</td>
              <td class="mono" style="font-size:10.5px">{m.vp.source ?? (m.vp.status === 'assumed' ? 'assumed' : '')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="sources">
      {
        Object.entries(materials.sources).map(([id, s]) => (
          <p>
            <span class="mono">{id}</span> {s.citation} <a href={s.url}>{s.doi ? `doi:${s.doi}` : 'link'}</a>
          </p>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="glossary">
    <h2>Two communities, one vocabulary</h2>
    <p class="sub">Seismologists and radar engineers rarely read each other. The terms this site uses from each, in plain words; they are underlined where they appear.</p>
    <dl class="glossary">
      {
        Object.values(GLOSSARY)
          .sort((a, b) => a.term.localeCompare(b.term))
          .map((e) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{e.term} <span class={`field ${e.field}`}>{e.field === 'both' ? 'seismic · radar' : e.field}</span></dt>
              <dd>{e.text}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
      }
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="run">
    <h2>Three commands rebuild everything</h2>
    <pre><code>cd sim && uv sync && uv run pytest                     # the simulation and its checks (~40 s)
uv run python experiments/p2_07_whole_chain.py         # any run, e.g. the whole chain (~5 min)
uv run python -m katabasis.export                      # everything the site reads
cd ../web && npm ci && npm run build                   # the site, into docs/</code></pre>
    <p class="sub">The lab’s Python package keeps its first name, <span class="mono">katabasis</span>. Real-image steps need the ICEYE products, which are not in the repository; their acquisition records are, so everything else reruns anywhere.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="layout">
    <h2>One repository, three parts</h2>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card"><h3>sites/</h3><p>The materials library and one composition per site, shared by the simulation and the viewer.</p></div>
      <div class="note-card"><h3>sim/</h3><p>The Python lab: compositions, the elastic solver, the arrays and inversions, and the first investigation kept intact in <span class="mono">sim/legacy/</span>.</p></div>
      <div class="note-card"><h3>web/</h3><p>This site: Astro and Three.js, hand-drawn figures, no chart library. <a href="https://github.com/ejhong/sar">Source</a> · <a href={`${base}/archive/`}>first investigation</a></p></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Doc>

<style>
  .glossary {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 12px 32px;
  }
  .glossary dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .glossary dd {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--body);
    margin-top: 2px;
  }
  .field {
    font: 9px var(--font-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-left: 6px;
  }
  .field.radar {
    color: var(--cinnabar);
  }
  .field.seismic {
    color: var(--ochre);
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .glossary {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  .check {
    font-size: 12.5px !important;
    color: var(--muted) !important;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 8px;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  .sources {
    margin-top: 14px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 6px;
  }
  .sources p {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--body);
  }
  .sources .mono {
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-right: 6px;
  }
  pre {
    background: var(--panel-bg);
    color: var(--panel-text);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 14px 16px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
</style>
